  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual overview of how particles move between the ocean, coast, and land through the atmosphere. It links three environments—offshore waters, the coastal zone, and terrestrial areas—and shows that particles can be emitted from surfaces, lifted upward, carried by wind, incorporated into clouds, and returned to the surface by deposition.\n\nOver the sea, waves generate “sea spray and bubble burst ejection,” which sends material into the air. On land and beaches, several surface processes release or move particles, including particle emission, resuspension, wind deflation, saltation, convection, and turbulent diffusion. Once airborne, particles are transported laterally by wind and may act as condensation or coalescing nuclei in clouds.\n\nThe figure also emphasizes removal pathways: wet deposition through rain and dry deposition directly onto surfaces. These pathways occur across marine, coastal, and terrestrial settings, suggesting a continuous exchange rather than isolated systems.\n\nA key message is the uneven state of knowledge. Blue labels indicate processes for which some studies, data, or methods exist, while orange labels mark processes that appear poorly studied or lack established methods. Overall, the image highlights a complex source–transport–deposition cycle and identifies major research gaps, especially in coastal and land-based particle dynamics.",
